title: Command transactions
tags: Commands
kind: reference
aliases: transaction txn mf.flush txn_flush
summary: A user command or a panel's command reads the workspace variables as they were when it began and its writes reach the workspaces all at once, when it ends — so no panel and no other command ever sees it half done.

A [[user command|User commands]], and a command a panel registers (or a generic
command run on a panel's functions, [[Generic panel commands]]), runs in a
''transaction'' over the [[workspace variables|Workspace variables]]:

* ''it reads a snapshot'': what `mf.workspace.get` / `all` answer is what the variables held when the command first read that workspace, plus what the command itself wrote. Another command, a panel or a script writing meanwhile does not move what it sees;
* ''its writes are held'' — `set`, `setMany`, `send` into any workspace — and reach the workspaces ''at once'' when it ends: every variable is in before any panel hears of one, and each is announced once.

A panel following several variables can therefore not read a command half done —
the race that made `metarecord-list:folder` run the old query and
`file-manager:reveal` lose its cursor, once in a few tries ([[Cross-panel selection]]).
Two commands running at once (two keys pressed quickly) do not see each other's
writes before they end, and neither waits for the other: nothing is queued, so a long
command holds up nothing.

!! Delivering before the end

What a transaction carries is the variables, the daemon calls, and the status bar and
message log. ''Anything else'' needs the variables delivered first, since a panel will
read them: opening or switching a panel, changing the layout or the focus, creating or
forking a workspace, running a command that is not a user command (a builtin, a
panel's command, a `!` shell line), asking the user. Before each of these the
transaction is ''flushed'': its writes are delivered, the GUI waits until their effect
is visible ([[When a command has finished]]), and the command reads a fresh snapshot
from then on. `mf.workspace.setMany(…)` followed by `mf.invoke(['panel:open', …])`
thus shows the panel on the new values.

`mf.flush()` does the same on demand, for a command that wants the user to see a step
before it goes on.

!! Nested commands

A user command invoked by another (`mf.invoke`) runs ''in the caller's
transaction'': it sees what the caller wrote, and the caller sees what it writes. Each
step of a recorded [[macro|Macros]] being run is its own command, so each starts after
the previous one is delivered.

A panel's command invoked by a user command runs after the caller's writes are
delivered, in the panel's transaction (below).

!! A panel's commands

A panel's command handler works through the `metafolder` object the panel was mounted
with, and so do the functions it calls: that object cannot tell which run a call
belongs to (JavaScript has no context that follows an `await`). The ''panel instance''
can: while one of its commands runs, ''everything that instance does'' through its
`metafolder` is in that run's transaction — the handler, the functions it calls, and
a listener or a timer of the panel firing meanwhile. So no handler is written
differently: `workspace.set` three times in a row reaches the workspace at once.

* Commands running at once on one instance share one transaction, ended after the last of them.
* A user command the panel invokes (`metafolder.invoke`) joins it, as from a user command.
* What a handler sets off and does not wait for — a slideshow's timer, after `file:play` has returned — is outside: the run has ended.
* A listener of the panel firing during a run reads the run's snapshot, not the value just announced to another key; it gets the announced value itself as its argument.

!! Conflicts and failures

When two transactions wrote the same variable, the one that ends last wins — for that
variable only; the others it did not write are left as they are. Nothing is replayed:
a command that wrote to the daemon or ran a program cannot be run again.

A command that fails or is abandoned still delivers what it wrote before, as a
command did before transactions: its daemon writes are not undone either.

A write into a workspace closed meanwhile is dropped.

!! Limits

* A builtin runs in no transaction: it writes directly, so one writing several variables a panel follows writes them with `setMany`.
* A panel's own writes outside its commands (a click moving the selection) and a script's `mf gui var` are not in any transaction: they are delivered at once — `setMany` is for them too.
* A snapshot is taken per workspace, at the first read of that workspace: a command reading two workspaces may see them at two moments.

!! How it is built

The transaction lives on the Rust side, which owns the variables (`GuiState`): a
command's API carries its transaction id — a user command's in its execution context,
a panel's in its instance's run scope (`lib/panels/runScope.ts`) — and the variable
calls made with it read the snapshot and add to the held writes. A snapshot costs no copy: a workspace's variables
are shared (`Arc`), and a write after a snapshot copies the table of pointers, not the
values.
